      <h2>Airlines Flying Nonstop from London to New York</h2>
      <p>The London–New York corridor is one of the most competitive routes in global aviation. Six or more airlines operate daily nonstop service, creating genuine price competition and a wide range of product options.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>British Airways</strong> — Multiple daily departures from LHR to JFK. BA operates its standard 777/787 fleet on this corridor. Club World business class is a flagship product.</li>
        <li><strong>Virgin Atlantic</strong> — Flies from both Heathrow and Gatwick to JFK. Known for a strong Upper Class cabin and competitive economy fares. Often the cheapest full-service option on this route.</li>
        <li><strong>American Airlines</strong> — Operates from LHR to JFK. Part of the oneworld alliance with BA.</li>
        <li><strong>Delta Air Lines</strong> — Operates from LHR and LGW to JFK. Delta One business class is a strong product on this route.</li>
        <li><strong>United Airlines</strong> — Primarily operates London–Newark (EWR), making it the go-to for United MileagePlus members.</li>
        <li><strong>Norse Atlantic</strong> — Budget long-haul carrier offering nonstop LGW–JFK at significantly lower prices than full-service competitors.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Flight Time: London to New York</h2>
      <p>The westbound flight from London to New York takes approximately 7–8 hours. The return eastbound flight is shorter at 6.5–7 hours, thanks to the jet stream working in your favor. Actual flight times may differ by up to 30–45 minutes depending on wind conditions.</p>
      <h2>Which Airports: LHR vs LGW, JFK vs EWR?</h2>
      <p><strong>From London:</strong> Most nonstop flights depart from Heathrow (LHR), which has more frequency and more airline choice. Gatwick (LGW) is primarily served by Virgin Atlantic and Norse Atlantic — and Gatwick fares are sometimes cheaper. Always check both when searching.</p>
      <p><strong>Into New York:</strong> JFK is the primary destination, though Newark (EWR) is served by United and sometimes offers cheaper fares. LaGuardia (LGA) does not serve transatlantic routes.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 <strong>Best time to book:</strong> For summer travel (June–August), book 4–6 months in advance. For winter travel outside holidays, last-minute fares often drop significantly, especially on Norse Atlantic.</p></div>
      <h2>Is There a Nonstop Business Class Option?</h2>
      <p>Yes — all full-service carriers offer business class. British Airways Club World, Virgin Atlantic Upper Class, Delta One, United Polaris, and American Business Class all compete on this route. This is one of the few transatlantic corridors where premium cabin award redemptions are reasonably accessible, particularly through partner programs.</p>
      <h2>Tips for Getting the Best Nonstop Fare</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Set a fare alert on Skyscanner or Google Flights — this route has frequent flash sales, particularly in January–February and October–November.</li>
        <li>Check Norse Atlantic and Level for budget nonstop options from Gatwick.</li>
        <li>Midweek departures (Tuesday, Wednesday) are almost always cheaper than weekend flights.</li>
        <li>If you have British Airways Avios, the LHR–JFK route can be redeemed for relatively few miles in off-peak periods.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Terminal Guide: London Heathrow and New York JFK</h2>
      <p><strong>At London Heathrow (LHR):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 5 (T5):</strong> British Airways — BA's home terminal. Modern, efficient, with the First Wing priority entrance for premium cabin passengers.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 3 (T3):</strong> Virgin Atlantic, American Airlines, Delta Air Lines (some services). Virgin's Clubhouse lounge at T3 is consistently rated among Heathrow's best.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 2 (T2):</strong> United Airlines and other Star Alliance carriers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At New York JFK:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 8 (T8):</strong> American Airlines and British Airways (as oneworld partners sharing a terminal).</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 4 (T4):</strong> Delta Air Lines and many international carriers including Virgin Atlantic (some services).</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 1 (T1):</strong> Air France, Lufthansa, and other foreign carriers — not relevant for LHR-JFK but useful context.</li>
        <li><strong>Newark Liberty (EWR):</strong> United Airlines operates from Terminal C. Connect to Manhattan via AirTrain and NJ Transit from EWR rail station in the terminal.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ JFK is undergoing a major terminal redevelopment. Terminal layouts and airline assignments can change — confirm your terminal at check-in or on your airline's app before traveling.</p></div>
      <h2>Baggage Allowances by Airline</h2>
      <p>Baggage policies differ by airline and fare class. Here is the standard economy allowance on the LHR–JFK nonstop for each major carrier:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>British Airways:</strong> Hand baggage included on all fares. 1 × 23kg checked bag included on World Traveller (economy) and above. Basic (Lite) fares exclude checked bags.</li>
        <li><strong>Virgin Atlantic:</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included in standard economy (Economy Classic and above). Economy Light excludes checked bags.</li>
        <li><strong>American Airlines:</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included on international economy fares. No carry-on fee for transatlantic.</li>
        <li><strong>Delta Air Lines:</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included on international economy. Delta Basic Economy excludes checked bags.</li>
        <li><strong>United Airlines:</strong> 1 × 23kg checked bag included on international economy fares (not on Basic Economy).</li>
        <li><strong>Norse Atlantic:</strong> Checked bags cost extra — approximately $50–80 per bag per direction. A 10kg carry-on bag is also charged on some fare classes.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Best Time to Book London to New York Nonstop</h2>
      <p><strong>Cheapest periods:</strong> January and February (post-holiday, low demand). October and early November. These months see the lowest nonstop economy fares on this route, sometimes 40–50% below summer peak pricing.</p>
      <p><strong>Most expensive periods:</strong> June through August (summer peak). Thanksgiving week (late November). Christmas and New Year's. Spring break (late March). For summer travel, booking 4–6 months in advance is strongly recommended.</p>
      <p><strong>Best booking window:</strong> For flexible travel, set a fare alert and book when prices drop below your target. For fixed dates in peak season, book as early as possible — seats at promotional prices are limited and sell fast.</p>
      <h2>Visa and Entry Requirements</h2>
      <p><strong>UK citizens traveling to the USA:</strong> UK passport holders can travel under the Visa Waiver Program (VWP) using an ESTA (Electronic System for Travel Authorization). Apply at esta.cbp.dhs.gov. Cost is $21 USD, valid for 2 years or until your passport expires. Apply at least 72 hours before departure. ESTA approval is usually instant but allow extra time.</p>
      <p><strong>US citizens traveling to the UK:</strong> US passport holders do not require a visa for tourism or business visits up to 6 months. From 2024, the UK introduced an Electronic Travel Authorisation (ETA) for US citizens — a pre-travel permission similar to ESTA, costing £10. Check gov.uk for current requirements as the rollout continues by nationality.</p>
      <p><strong>Other nationalities:</strong> Requirements vary. Non-UK, non-EU travelers departing from London may need a US visa. Travelers arriving in the UK from the US who are not UK or Irish citizens may need an ETA or visa. Check current requirements for your specific passport at least 2 months before travel.</p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Which is the cheapest airline for nonstop London–New York?</h3>
      <p>Norse Atlantic consistently offers the lowest base fares on the LGW–JFK nonstop route. However, once checked bags, meals, and seat selection are added, the price gap versus full-service carriers narrows. On flash sale days, Virgin Atlantic and British Airways sometimes match or beat Norse's all-in price. Always compare the total cost including fees.</p>
      <h3>Can I use British Airways Avios for this route?</h3>
      <p>Yes. BA Avios can be redeemed on BA and American Airlines flights on this route. Off-peak Club World (business class) awards on BA start from 50,000 Avios each way. Note that BA charges carrier-imposed surcharges; redeeming Avios on American Airlines for the same route incurs lower fees. Check the BA Executive Club website for current off-peak dates.</p>
      <h3>Is JFK or EWR better for visiting New York City?</h3>
      <p>For most visitors staying in Manhattan, both airports are comparably convenient. JFK connects via AirTrain to the NYC subway (E, J, Z lines to Manhattan) — about 45–60 minutes to Midtown. Newark connects via AirTrain and NJ Transit to Penn Station — about 30–40 minutes to Midtown. EWR is often faster to reach Midtown, while JFK is better for outer boroughs and downtown. If United Airlines is your carrier, EWR is your airport.</p>
      <h3>What is the flight time from Gatwick to JFK vs Heathrow to JFK?</h3>
      <p>Flight times are essentially identical — approximately 7–8 hours westbound and 6.5–7 hours eastbound. The routing difference between LGW and LHR is negligible for this destination. The airport choice is about airline preference and price, not journey time in the air.</p>
      <h2>In-Flight Experience: Economy Class Comparison</h2>
      <p><strong>British Airways World Traveller:</strong> Standard narrow seats on 777 and 787 fleet. Entertainment system is comprehensive. On-demand catering on most services. Meal quality has improved in recent years but remains a point of discussion among frequent flyers.</p>
      <p><strong>Virgin Atlantic Economy:</strong> Consistently rated better than BA for economy class product quality. Slightly wider seats on some aircraft configurations. The Virgin Atlantic app entertainment system is well-regarded. Crew service culture tends to be warmer and more engaging than legacy competitors.</p>
      <p><strong>Delta Economy:</strong> Delta's economy cabin on transatlantic routes is competitive, with seatback entertainment and reasonable seat pitch. Delta's on-time performance record on this route is strong.</p>
      <p><strong>Norse Atlantic Economy:</strong> 787-9 Dreamliner cabin with improved pressurization and humidity. Narrow seat pitch in standard rows. No in-seat entertainment — bring your own device with downloaded content. Food and beverages purchased separately. Good aircraft, minimal frills.</p>
